From 308e9fc348a95c19d4ab87b7784c93d98a6a955e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sean Lin <seanlinmt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:53:12 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] feat(core): short-request lanes, a KV cap on long admissions,
  bounded pool-blocked bypass

ShortLaneConfig (read at LaneHost.init) replaces 0070's two switches:
TF_DS_SHORT_RESERVE_LANES (default 1, clamped to lanes - 1), TF_DS_SHORT_MAX_TOKENS
(default 6144: uncached prompt + short-lane decode bound), TF_SHORT_TOTAL_CTX_MAX
(default 32768: whole prompt), TF_SHORT_DEMOTE_TOKENS (default 512), TF_ACTIVE_KV_CAP
(default 786432), TF_BYPASS_MAX_SKIPS (default 4) and TF_KV_ALLOC_STEP (default 512).
0 turns a bound off.

A request is short when its uncached prompt fits cut_in (when set), its prompt fits
TF_SHORT_TOTAL_CTX_MAX and uncached prompt + min(max_tokens, TF_SHORT_DEMOTE_TOKENS)
(or max_tokens when demotion is off) fits TF_DS_SHORT_MAX_TOKENS; the qualification
no longer depends on a reserved lane. Long requests may not take the reserved lanes,
and, with other streams admitted, are held while the admitted streams' KV estimates
(max(prompt, context) + TF_KV_ALLOC_STEP of the decode left) and their own pass
TF_ACTIVE_KV_CAP. A short stream that has decoded TF_SHORT_DEMOTE_TOKENS counts as
long. The cap and the step are admission accounting only: the backend's pool
allocates as before.

A request refused for room (ContextFull, or a restore waitRoom refuses) is marked
pool_blocked and waits at the front (full_wait) as before; short requests behind it
are admitted past it at once, TF_BYPASS_MAX_SKIPS of them at most per wait (a pass
then refused for room counts too). A refused bypass goes back behind the waiting
requests and leaves full_wait/full_retry as they are, so it never delays the front.
pool_blocked clears with full_wait (fewer streams, full_retry, or the front
cancelled), while bypass_skips resets only when active streams drop, the front
request is cancelled, or the retried front request succeeds. When a front request in
ready or queued is held from TF_ACTIVE_KV_CAP specifically because of admitted short
streams (capBlockedByShort: either its KV estimate exceeds the cap with only short
streams active, or it would fit alongside admitted long streams alone), short
bypasses past it are likewise bounded by TF_BYPASS_MAX_SKIPS (cap_wait_id /
cap_skips), clearing when the front runs, is cancelled, or is no longer blocked by
short streams, so >TF_ACTIVE_KV_CAP or short-KV-blocked long requests do not starve.
Woken restores in ready are scanned in order with the same cut-in and admission
checks before falling through to queued.
